00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.876 align:middle line:90%


00:00:20.876 --> 00:00:23.778 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Anyway, today we
have a guest speaker.

00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:24.742 align:middle line:90%
His name is Abe Stein.

00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:28.116 align:middle line:84%
He's the audio director for the
Singapore MIT Initiative.

00:00:28.116 --> 00:00:35.370 align:middle line:84%
And he's going to run through
brainstorming exercises.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.216 align:middle line:84%
We're not actually planning out
the game that you're going

00:00:38.216 --> 00:00:42.040 align:middle line:84%
to be doing for your first
assignment in this class.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.952 align:middle line:84%
This is a-- you're
doing other--

00:00:43.952 --> 00:00:46.360 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, these will
just be practice.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.962 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So basically, there
will be a group activity

00:00:49.962 --> 00:00:52.520 align:middle line:84%
involved in this, but the things
that you're working on,

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.574 align:middle line:84%
those have to be the same
groups that you will

00:00:55.574 --> 00:00:57.890 align:middle line:84%
eventually be using for
your first assignment.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.440 align:middle line:84%
The idea is just to get
used to the process of

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.820 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming and some of the
general rules of thumb.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.981 align:middle line:84%
And before I spoil
the entire talk--

00:01:05.981 --> 00:01:06.670 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: That's OK.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.930 align:middle line:90%
So my name is Abe Stein.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:12.460 align:middle line:84%
A little bit of background
on myself.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.290 align:middle line:90%
I am the audio director here.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:19.789 align:middle line:84%
I've been working in sound
design officially for a number

00:01:19.789 --> 00:01:22.333 align:middle line:84%
of years now, five
some odd years.

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:28.340 align:middle line:84%
Unofficially, way longer than
that doing TV and video game

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.280 align:middle line:90%
sound design.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.890 align:middle line:84%
And then before that, I was
a high school teacher.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:39.980 align:middle line:84%
And so I think to the extent
that talking about creative

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.850 align:middle line:84%
collaboration, I have a fair
amount of experience ranging

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.800 align:middle line:84%
even back to when I was teaching
high school, for

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:51.990 align:middle line:84%
trying to encourage what we
might call organized ideation.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.125 align:middle line:84%
I guess first off, I'll thank
you guys for reading the text.

00:01:56.125 --> 00:01:57.060 align:middle line:90%
It is funny.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.320 align:middle line:84%
It does have these I guess now
a little antiquated, 1950s

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.010 align:middle line:90%
references.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:10.550 align:middle line:84%
And some of his scientific
evidence for why brainstorming

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.890 align:middle line:84%
is good is not particularly
well-supported.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.103 align:middle line:90%
He's like, we got 95 ideas.

00:02:16.103 --> 00:02:18.075 align:middle line:84%
And it's like, well how many
ideas did you get doing

00:02:18.075 --> 00:02:18.568 align:middle line:90%
it the other way?

00:02:18.568 --> 00:02:20.047 align:middle line:90%
He doesn't tell you.

00:02:20.047 --> 00:02:23.010 align:middle line:84%
But hey, 95, that's
a big number.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.880 align:middle line:84%
So a little bit of background
about Alex Osborn.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.306 align:middle line:84%
He was an advertising
executive.

00:02:29.306 --> 00:02:34.180 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see where
brainstorming and advertising

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.250 align:middle line:84%
is a creative industry,
as a design industry.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.250 align:middle line:84%
And so getting multiple people
in interdisciplinary different

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.940 align:middle line:84%
skills working together on a
creative project, it could be

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:45.730 align:middle line:90%
a challenge.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.212 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see how
brainstorming came out of the

00:02:49.212 --> 00:02:51.140 align:middle line:84%
work he was doing
in advertising

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.550 align:middle line:84%
So just to get started, I would
say you've read-- and so

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.940 align:middle line:84%
you have a sense-- but forget
what you think you know

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.380 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming is.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.700 align:middle line:84%
Some of what you think
it is will apply.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.340 align:middle line:84%
Some of the most fundamental
principles to brainstorming

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.447 align:middle line:84%
maybe you don't realize, or you
didn't realize until you

00:03:08.447 --> 00:03:08.650 align:middle line:90%
did the reading.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.955 align:middle line:84%
And that's a little bit of what
I want to talk about.

00:03:10.955 --> 00:03:13.440 align:middle line:84%
I think the foundation for his
brainstorming ideas is

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.560 align:middle line:90%
actually really solid.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.464 align:middle line:84%
It's something that we
should explore today.

00:03:17.464 --> 00:03:20.370 align:middle line:90%


00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.260 align:middle line:84%
Why brainstorming for
video game design is

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:24.340 align:middle line:90%
an interesting question.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.169 align:middle line:84%
But I touched on that a little
bit with its relation to

00:03:27.169 --> 00:03:27.400 align:middle line:90%
advertizing, right?

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.760 align:middle line:84%
I mean we're talking about a
creative industry where you

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.640 align:middle line:84%
have people with various skill
sets, whether they're artists

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.890 align:middle line:84%
or sound designers
or game designers

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.720 align:middle line:90%
explicitly or producers.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.190 align:middle line:84%
All of these people
existing in a

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.320 align:middle line:90%
collaborative environment together.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.070 align:middle line:84%
They're bringing different
things to the table.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:45.200 align:middle line:84%
How do you get them to
work together and be

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.290 align:middle line:90%
collaboratively creative?

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.980 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a first
step in that process.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.818 align:middle line:84%
And so this is why brainstorming
is particularly

00:03:51.818 --> 00:03:53.270 align:middle line:90%
useful for game design.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.630 align:middle line:84%
And then the other thing that
I think is important to

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:57.280 align:middle line:90%
understand--

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.660 align:middle line:84%
and we'll talk about
it a bit more--

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:03.980 align:middle line:84%
is the social pressures that
are created in professional

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.360 align:middle line:84%
creative environments, even
non-professional actually.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.060 align:middle line:84%
I mean it would exist in an
educational environment too.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.510 align:middle line:90%
You guys are all at MIT.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.370 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the most prestigious
universities in

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.890 align:middle line:90%
the world, right?

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:20.990 align:middle line:84%
And so you're probably no
stranger to the idea of social

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:25.470 align:middle line:84%
pressure in the process
of ideation, right?

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.795 align:middle line:84%
You sit there and you say, if I
say this idea, am I going to

00:04:27.795 --> 00:04:29.660 align:middle line:90%
look like an idiot?

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:34.780 align:middle line:84%
And usually the outcome from
that is, if I don't say

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.870 align:middle line:84%
anything, maybe I don't get the
gain of presenting a good

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.195 align:middle line:84%
idea, but I don't get the hit
of making a bad idea.

00:04:41.195 --> 00:04:43.070 align:middle line:90%
So it's self-preservation.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.400 align:middle line:84%
Why take the risk if
it's not worth it.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.690 align:middle line:84%
And so I think MIT is an
interesting place to talk

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.750 align:middle line:84%
about brainstorming and this
kind of organized ideation

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:57.210 align:middle line:84%
because there is social and
academic pressure here too,

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.160 align:middle line:84%
pressure to be right, pressure
to not say something that

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.290 align:middle line:90%
would seem wrong.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:06.650 align:middle line:84%
And that sort of pressure is
completely antithetical to the

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.750 align:middle line:84%
idea of a brainstorming
session.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.790 align:middle line:84%
And so that's a basis for why
not only it's good for video

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.630 align:middle line:84%
games, but why it's also good
to talk about here at MIT.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:22.070 align:middle line:84%
So there are four basic
principles of brainstorming.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:23.880 align:middle line:84%
These were outlined
by Alex Osborn.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:30.905 align:middle line:84%
He was an ad agent, and he says
it was 1938 when he first

00:05:30.905 --> 00:05:31.476 align:middle line:90%
started using it.

00:05:31.476 --> 00:05:34.420 align:middle line:84%
People started talking
about it since then.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.670 align:middle line:84%
And I like when he said that
it spiraled out of control,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.386 align:middle line:90%
that people ran away with it.

00:05:39.386 --> 00:05:41.090 align:middle line:84%
They were like, oh, this
is a great idea.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.730 align:middle line:84%
But then they were practicing
it wrong.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:46.810 align:middle line:84%
And so in applied imaginations,
which is the

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.785 align:middle line:84%
text you guys read, it's
him reflecting on this

00:05:50.785 --> 00:05:53.793 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming idea and saying
this is really how it should

00:05:53.793 --> 00:05:56.010 align:middle line:90%
be exercised.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.730 align:middle line:84%
The first principle is
single-handedly the most

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:04.400 align:middle line:84%
important principle, and
that's no criticism.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:12.276 align:middle line:84%
That's a blanket way of saying
it, but really in a more broad

00:06:12.276 --> 00:06:16.200 align:middle line:84%
sense, it's saying judgement
needs to be excluded.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.860 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, it's both
positive and negative, but

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.980 align:middle line:84%
especially you don't want to
criticize each other's work.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:25.886 align:middle line:84%
You also don't want to spend
time during a brainstorming

00:06:25.886 --> 00:06:29.330 align:middle line:84%
session talking about how
good an idea is, right?

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.486 align:middle line:84%
If someone throws out an idea,
and someone's like, oh, this

00:06:33.486 --> 00:06:33.690 align:middle line:90%
is the best idea.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:34.980 align:middle line:84%
We don't even need to
keep going anymore.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:36.910 align:middle line:90%
Then it's like, whoa, no.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.710 align:middle line:84%
Wait, now we're not
brainstorming anymore.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:44.140 align:middle line:84%
And so really judgement is
absolutely supposed to be

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:45.390 align:middle line:90%
excluded from the process.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.680 align:middle line:90%


00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:52.530 align:middle line:84%
I think that this is the primary
principle that goes

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.010 align:middle line:84%
against that social
pressure, right?

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:55.910 align:middle line:84%
Because the social pressure of
judgment, the people who are

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:57.900 align:middle line:84%
saying what you have, an
executive who's in charge of

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.612 align:middle line:84%
you, a boss who's saying whether
or not the ideas are

00:06:59.612 --> 00:07:01.510 align:middle line:84%
good or bad, or whether you
have colleagues that are

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.740 align:middle line:84%
saying whether or not ideas are
good or bad, that sort of

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.500 align:middle line:84%
social pressure that forces
people to stay quiet and maybe

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:10.900 align:middle line:84%
hold onto good ideas or
throw away good ideas.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:15.498 align:middle line:84%
If everybody's on board with a
lack of criticism, then you

00:07:15.498 --> 00:07:18.426 align:middle line:84%
have more freedom to
express yourself.

00:07:18.426 --> 00:07:24.282 align:middle line:84%
Just pulling back to the text
a little bit, there was this

00:07:24.282 --> 00:07:28.300 align:middle line:84%
great quote from this vice
president I'll read to you

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.160 align:middle line:90%
guys again.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.150 align:middle line:84%
He said, "It was hard to get
through my head what you were

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.110 align:middle line:90%
trying to do with us.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.080 align:middle line:84%
My 15 years of conference after
conference in my company

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.860 align:middle line:84%
have conditioned me against
shooting wild.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.710 align:middle line:84%
Almost all of us officers
rate each other on

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:41.200 align:middle line:90%
the basis of judgment.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.650 align:middle line:84%
We are far more apt to look up
to the other fellow if he

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.530 align:middle line:84%
makes no mistakes than if he
suggests lots of ideas." That

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.250 align:middle line:84%
is something that may sound
familiar to you.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.620 align:middle line:84%
"So I've always kept myself from
spouting any suggestions

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:51.930 align:middle line:90%
which could be sneered at.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.633 align:middle line:84%
I wish our people would feel
free to shoot ideas the way we

00:07:54.633 --> 00:07:55.892 align:middle line:84%
have been doing in these
brainstorm sessions.

00:07:55.892 --> 00:08:00.250 align:middle line:84%
And so really, this lack of
criticism, this creating a

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.060 align:middle line:84%
safe environment for ideation,
is the most fundamental

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.690 align:middle line:84%
principle in this
brainstorming.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:10.600 align:middle line:84%
And it really goes right at
those social pressures which

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.726 align:middle line:84%
can stop creativity and really
hamper creativity.

00:08:14.726 --> 00:08:16.678 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about that?

00:08:16.678 --> 00:08:20.582 align:middle line:84%
That makes sense right, this
principle of no criticism.

00:08:20.582 --> 00:08:22.046 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:08:22.046 --> 00:08:26.960 align:middle line:84%
Principle #2, freewheeling, as
Mr. Osborn likes to call it.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:27.990 align:middle line:90%
The wilder idea the better.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:32.000 align:middle line:84%
This is also hard
to get used to.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.895 align:middle line:84%
Again, it was like the guy
said in that quote.

00:08:34.895 --> 00:08:37.310 align:middle line:84%
You resist the urge to throw
out something that's

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.070 align:middle line:90%
absolutely crazy and absolutely

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:40.309 align:middle line:90%
wild for fear of ridicule.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:43.730 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we eliminate the fear
of ridicule, and we understand

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.150 align:middle line:84%
that we're just trying to get
every idea that we can, then

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:49.662 align:middle line:84%
freewheeling is absolutely
encouraged.

00:08:49.662 --> 00:08:54.076 align:middle line:84%
And you need to get passed that
oh, this will never work.

00:08:54.076 --> 00:08:56.372 align:middle line:84%
Or nobody would ever
like this.

00:08:56.372 --> 00:09:03.020 align:middle line:84%
And I put up a katamari up here
because that's the sort

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:06.167 align:middle line:84%
of idea that someone throws
out at a meeting, and if

00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:08.490 align:middle line:84%
there's criticism, someone
will be like, that's the

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.045 align:middle line:90%
dumbest thing I ever heard.

00:09:09.045 --> 00:09:11.760 align:middle line:84%
You can't just roll around
picking up stuff for the king

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:12.225 align:middle line:90%
of the cosmos.

00:09:12.225 --> 00:09:15.015 align:middle line:90%
That's insane.

00:09:15.015 --> 00:09:19.380 align:middle line:84%
And it probably was a synthesis
of a lot of

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.680 align:middle line:84%
different ideas that created
that game, which we'll talk

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:22.510 align:middle line:90%
about later.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:26.610 align:middle line:84%
But really, having these wild,
crazy ideas, you may think

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:27.550 align:middle line:90%
it's over the edge.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.370 align:middle line:90%
And it might be over the edge.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:33.670 align:middle line:84%
But it is way easier to make
something feasible than it is

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.760 align:middle line:84%
to take something that's pretty
normal and make it

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.090 align:middle line:90%
innovative and interesting.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.600 align:middle line:84%
It's much easier to go in
that backward direction.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.440 align:middle line:84%
So really the wilder the
idea you can come

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.110 align:middle line:90%
up with, the stronger.

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:51.276 align:middle line:90%


00:09:51.276 --> 00:09:53.711 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about
principle #2?

00:09:53.711 --> 00:09:54.198 align:middle line:90%
Making sense?

00:09:54.198 --> 00:09:54.685 align:middle line:90%
On board?

00:09:54.685 --> 00:09:56.150 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.730 align:middle line:90%
Quantity.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:02.810 align:middle line:84%
This is really the goal of
a brainstorming session.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:06.280 align:middle line:84%
Come up with as many ideas
as you can, right?

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.770 align:middle line:84%
It stands to reason that if
you have lots of ideas,

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:11.590 align:middle line:84%
there's a greater likelihood
that you'll have a useful one.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:14.885 align:middle line:84%
That if, say, you have two,
your choices are limited.

00:10:14.885 --> 00:10:16.750 align:middle line:84%
You're going to pick the better
of the two, I suppose,

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:19.380 align:middle line:84%
but it might not be the
best idea you have.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.140 align:middle line:84%
And so really, this adds onto
that wild idea, throw

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:24.100 align:middle line:90%
everything out you can.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:27.060 align:middle line:84%
You need to just get everything
out and everything

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:32.000 align:middle line:84%
on the page in order to even be
able to start judging it.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:35.130 align:middle line:84%
And that's the important thing
too that needs to be clarified

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.560 align:middle line:84%
is that brainstorming
is a first step

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:38.700 align:middle line:90%
in a creative process.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.680 align:middle line:90%
It's not the whole process.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.016 align:middle line:84%
It's not like, oh, we have this
creative problem that we

00:10:43.016 --> 00:10:43.215 align:middle line:90%
need to solve.

00:10:43.215 --> 00:10:45.630 align:middle line:84%
We'll have a brainstorming
session, then we'll have it.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.030 align:middle line:84%
And actually, that's something
that hampers

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:48.230 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming a lot.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:49.570 align:middle line:84%
You have a brainstorming
session.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:50.990 align:middle line:84%
You come up with these
great ideas.

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:53.930 align:middle line:84%
And then everybody leaves, and
you never return to it or you

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.990 align:middle line:84%
never reflect on which these
ideas is going to be best, or

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:57.990 align:middle line:90%
you just end up picking one.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:03.520 align:middle line:84%
And so the judgment needs to
happen as well, but not now.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.440 align:middle line:84%
In the brainstorm session, it's
just get everything out

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:06.690 align:middle line:90%
that you can.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.440 align:middle line:90%


00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.450 align:middle line:84%
And really it just
stands to reason.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.145 align:middle line:84%
And I made fun of Alex Osborn
when he was saying, well, in

00:11:14.145 --> 00:11:16.170 align:middle line:84%
this instance in the government,
they had 95 ideas.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.670 align:middle line:84%
And we came up with eight
new [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:20.670 align:middle line:84%
How many did you come
up with before?

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:21.440 align:middle line:90%
But it's true.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.225 align:middle line:84%
The more you have, the better
you're going to do.

00:11:25.225 --> 00:11:26.120 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:28.590 align:middle line:84%
And then this one is related to
that, which is the idea of

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:31.575 align:middle line:84%
synthesis, or building
on ideas.

00:11:31.575 --> 00:11:34.210 align:middle line:84%
Which is that oftentimes the
best idea, the best creative

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:37.820 align:middle line:84%
idea, is not going to be
something that's just a unique

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:41.426 align:middle line:84%
idea that stands alone in this
corpus of ideas that you've

00:11:41.426 --> 00:11:42.950 align:middle line:84%
collected over the brainstorming
It's going to be

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:46.315 align:middle line:84%
an idea that is a synthesis
of one idea an another.

00:11:46.315 --> 00:11:48.980 align:middle line:84%
Or someone suggests an idea, and
then that makes you think

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:50.240 align:middle line:84%
of something else that's
related to it.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.610 align:middle line:90%
And you build on that.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:54.270 align:middle line:84%
And you can see, in really
positive, good brainstorming

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:56.170 align:middle line:90%
sessions, there's a momentum.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.150 align:middle line:84%
And one of the things that can
really hurt that momentum is

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:00.190 align:middle line:84%
when people are starting to
riff on an idea, and then

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:05.180 align:middle line:84%
someone is like, oh, but in
other news, we could do this.

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.990 align:middle line:84%
And so it's really encouraged
that you synthesize ideas.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.955 align:middle line:84%
If you have that other
left field idea,

00:12:09.955 --> 00:12:10.930 align:middle line:90%
don't throw it away.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.770 align:middle line:84%
Jot it down so that you can
bring it up later, but don't

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:17.500 align:middle line:84%
kill the momentum of the
synthesis if that's happening.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:20.670 align:middle line:84%
It's really important
that you start--

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:23.980 align:middle line:84%
it's the collaborative part
of this creative ideation.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:27.265 align:middle line:84%
It's this combining of
ideas that's going

00:12:27.265 --> 00:12:28.984 align:middle line:90%
to create good ideas.

00:12:28.984 --> 00:12:32.580 align:middle line:84%
And I think we can take that
as a given, that if you

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.084 align:middle line:84%
collect a bunch of skilled,
thoughtful, creative people

00:12:36.084 --> 00:12:39.060 align:middle line:84%
together, combining their
ideas is going to create

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:41.859 align:middle line:84%
something that's better than
just any one idea that one

00:12:41.859 --> 00:12:43.585 align:middle line:84%
creative professional
is going to have.

00:12:43.585 --> 00:12:47.775 align:middle line:84%
Or at least just a different
solution that might be more

00:12:47.775 --> 00:12:49.501 align:middle line:90%
ideal given the problem.

00:12:49.501 --> 00:12:53.116 align:middle line:84%
And so you can think of things
like, Lego is pretty awesome.

00:12:53.116 --> 00:12:56.950 align:middle line:84%
Star Wars is also
pretty awesome.

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:00.550 align:middle line:90%
But Lego Star Wars [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:01.550 align:middle line:90%
is also awesome.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:04.830 align:middle line:90%
Similarly, Marvel is dope.

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.768 align:middle line:90%
Capcom is also pretty dope.

00:13:06.768 --> 00:13:10.184 align:middle line:84%
Marvel Versus Capcom is
tremendously dope.

00:13:10.184 --> 00:13:13.920 align:middle line:84%
So building on ideas can
create obviously really

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:15.240 align:middle line:90%
interesting things.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.890 align:middle line:84%
And it's a good idea to keep
working on that in your

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.396 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming sessions, what
can you put together?

00:13:20.396 --> 00:13:23.330 align:middle line:90%


00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:24.890 align:middle line:90%
So how is it organized?

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:26.390 align:middle line:84%
We talked about the
basic principles.

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:27.870 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about those
four principles?

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:28.800 align:middle line:90%
Did they all make sense?

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.868 align:middle line:84%
Did they all seem to
be grounded in--

00:13:31.868 --> 00:13:32.360 align:middle line:90%
you're nodding yes.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.328 align:middle line:84%
It's the greatest thing
I've ever heard.

00:13:34.328 --> 00:13:37.290 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:41.186 align:middle line:84%
So the question then becomes,
how do you organize your

00:13:41.186 --> 00:13:42.120 align:middle line:90%
brainstorm.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:45.110 align:middle line:84%
One of the points that Alex
Osborn makes is that it needs

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:47.390 align:middle line:84%
to have a casual spirit or
a casual atmosphere.

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:49.022 align:middle line:84%
He refers to it as a
picnic, which is

00:13:49.022 --> 00:13:49.350 align:middle line:90%
another one of those--

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.300 align:middle line:84%
I haven't been on a picnic
in a really long time.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:56.035 align:middle line:84%
But it's like, we brought out
lunches in, and then we talked

00:13:56.035 --> 00:13:56.138 align:middle line:90%
afterwards.

00:13:56.138 --> 00:13:57.635 align:middle line:84%
We call those lunch
meetings now.

00:13:57.635 --> 00:14:00.629 align:middle line:90%


00:14:00.629 --> 00:14:04.490 align:middle line:84%
But he has a good point that
if a large part of the

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:08.010 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming session is
intended to combat social

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:11.100 align:middle line:84%
pressures that stop you from
being creative and inhibit

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.080 align:middle line:84%
your creativity, then it would
stand to reason that creating

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:18.570 align:middle line:84%
a casual spirit, a casual
environment for this process

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:19.450 align:middle line:90%
is important.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:26.600 align:middle line:84%
And so most video game processes
and most video game

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.856 align:middle line:84%
companies have a pretty casual
spirit and environment because

00:14:28.856 --> 00:14:30.583 align:middle line:84%
of the nature of the
work in general.

00:14:30.583 --> 00:14:32.650 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes this might
not be that hard.

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:35.070 align:middle line:84%
But things to consider
are, what is the

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.170 align:middle line:90%
makeup of your team?

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:41.060 align:middle line:84%
If your president of the company
does a really bad job

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:43.860 align:middle line:84%
of putting himself or herself
on the same level as all the

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:46.846 align:middle line:84%
people, you might want to
exclude that person from the

00:14:46.846 --> 00:14:47.812 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming session.

00:14:47.812 --> 00:14:50.871 align:middle line:84%
Because then everybody is going
to feel the pressure of

00:14:50.871 --> 00:14:51.193 align:middle line:90%
the president.

00:14:51.193 --> 00:14:51.796 align:middle line:84%
And oh, do we need to
make an idea that

00:14:51.796 --> 00:14:53.130 align:middle line:90%
makes that person happiest.

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:55.500 align:middle line:84%
You have to think to think these
things through in terms

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.788 align:middle line:84%
of what the environment is
going to be for your

00:14:57.788 --> 00:15:00.320 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming session
and who's involved.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:04.379 align:middle line:84%
So it is an important point
and an important step.

00:15:04.379 --> 00:15:09.290 align:middle line:84%
Starting with a bad or a
worthless idea encourages that

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:12.320 align:middle line:90%
freewheeling sense.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.240 align:middle line:84%
A lot of times, people
will contribute

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:16.440 align:middle line:90%
ideas that feel safe.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.110 align:middle line:84%
Again, you want to come
up with the most

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:19.790 align:middle line:90%
ideas that you can.

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:23.940 align:middle line:84%
So having someone with the
courage, or even bringing to

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:28.530 align:middle line:84%
the beginning of it a crazy,
worthless, bad, freewheeling

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:32.400 align:middle line:84%
idea can help encourage people
to keep doing that.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:35.670 align:middle line:84%
So either starting with some who
has a crazy idea or even

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:39.273 align:middle line:84%
bringing it to the brainstorm at
the very beginning can be a

00:15:39.273 --> 00:15:39.980 align:middle line:90%
really good thing as well.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.565 align:middle line:90%
This is a key one.

00:15:41.565 --> 00:15:44.400 align:middle line:90%
Don't interrupt anyone.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:46.820 align:middle line:84%
It's really important in an
brainstorming session to

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:48.850 align:middle line:84%
encourage that every
voice is heard.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.580 align:middle line:90%


00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:56.112 align:middle line:84%
Let me see if I can find that
quote from that one guy.

00:15:56.112 --> 00:15:58.320 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, here we go.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:15:59.550 align:middle line:90%
It's great.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:02.400 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Think up or shut up.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.240 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, there's that
one too, which is a good one.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:08.172 align:middle line:84%
But it's talking about "the
spirit of a brainstorm session

00:16:08.172 --> 00:16:09.190 align:middle line:90%
is important.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.300 align:middle line:84%
Self-encouragement is needed
almost as much as mutual

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:12.735 align:middle line:90%
encouragement.

00:16:12.735 --> 00:16:15.930 align:middle line:84%
A perfectionism complex will
throttle effort and abort

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:19.090 align:middle line:84%
ideas." He says, "One of the
ablest members kept mum

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:20.270 align:middle line:84%
throughout one of
our sessions.

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:20.840 align:middle line:90%
I button-holed him"--

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:22.280 align:middle line:90%
never heard that expression--

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:25.180 align:middle line:84%
"afterward and begged him to
spout whatever ideas might

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:26.040 align:middle line:84%
come to his mind in
our next meeting."

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:27.200 align:middle line:84%
And this is where
it gets good.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:30.450 align:middle line:84%
This is the really able member
of this ad team.

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:31.960 align:middle line:90%
He says, all right, I'll try.

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:33.490 align:middle line:90%
But here's what happened.

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.800 align:middle line:84%
"After our first meeting, I
jotted down about 15 ideas

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:36.974 align:middle line:84%
with the thought that
I would bring

00:16:36.974 --> 00:16:38.120 align:middle line:90%
them to our next session.

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:39.595 align:middle line:84%
When I looked them over, I
decided that they were

00:16:39.595 --> 00:16:42.728 align:middle line:84%
worthless, so I just
tore up the list."

00:16:42.728 --> 00:16:45.652 align:middle line:84%
And Alex goes on to to say it
took awhile to encourage this

00:16:45.652 --> 00:16:47.360 align:middle line:84%
guy to understand that there's
no such thing as a worthless

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:50.920 align:middle line:84%
idea, that it can either be
expanded upon or revised.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:54.340 align:middle line:84%
And that's that synthesis
thing, that

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:56.495 align:middle line:90%
having a wild idea.

00:16:56.495 --> 00:16:58.910 align:middle line:84%
And getting back to this idea
of the interruption, nothing

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:03.390 align:middle line:84%
will stop a person from wanting
to contribute more

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.970 align:middle line:84%
than not being to even have
their voice be heard.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:07.485 align:middle line:84%
They're trying to say something,
and then they just

00:17:07.485 --> 00:17:09.319 align:middle line:84%
get cut out by somebody else
who is like, oh, this is

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:12.460 align:middle line:84%
better, especially for a shy
person or a person who is

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:14.380 align:middle line:84%
otherwise feeling like my
ideas are worthless.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:16.540 align:middle line:84%
They won't be able to
get it out because

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:20.060 align:middle line:90%
they've been cut short.

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:23.130 align:middle line:84%
So some of this responsibility
falls on what we might call

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:27.164 align:middle line:84%
the leader, which I'll talk
about later, of the group.

00:17:27.164 --> 00:17:31.413 align:middle line:84%
But it's a collective
responsibility that everybody

00:17:31.413 --> 00:17:35.832 align:middle line:84%
makes sure that as someone is
expressing an idea, that it's

00:17:35.832 --> 00:17:37.305 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:17:37.305 --> 00:17:40.742 align:middle line:84%
And to that, keep
the description

00:17:40.742 --> 00:17:43.700 align:middle line:90%
of your idea short.

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:45.950 align:middle line:84%
If people are sitting here, and
you've now hijacked the

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:47.520 align:middle line:84%
30-minute brainstorming session
with your one idea

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.730 align:middle line:84%
that took five minutes
to explain, that can

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:51.770 align:middle line:90%
also hamper it, right?

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.170 align:middle line:84%
Part of it is not only don't
interrupt people, but also

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:56.960 align:middle line:84%
make sure that when you are
contributing, you are allowing

00:17:56.960 --> 00:17:59.672 align:middle line:84%
for other people to contribute
as well, something that maybe

00:17:59.672 --> 00:18:02.180 align:middle line:90%
is not considered as much.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.260 align:middle line:84%
For the organization, it
does need a leader.

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:06.410 align:middle line:84%
I hate that term, but that's
what he used in the book.

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.995 align:middle line:84%
I tend to use the term clerk,
which is a Quaker idea, which

00:18:08.995 --> 00:18:10.520 align:middle line:90%
I grab onto.

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.050 align:middle line:84%
A clerk being more
of a facilitator

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:13.440 align:middle line:90%
than an actual leader.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:20.980 align:middle line:84%
Leader suggests boss, which is
not great for everyone's even

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:22.444 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming session.

00:18:22.444 --> 00:18:24.590 align:middle line:84%
And then of course the
secretary, or as he calls it,

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:28.566 align:middle line:84%
an associate leader, this is
someone who writes stuff down.

00:18:28.566 --> 00:18:31.572 align:middle line:84%
There's some of that good,
old-fashioned hierarchy going

00:18:31.572 --> 00:18:33.860 align:middle line:90%
on in this text.

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.470 align:middle line:84%
But obviously, we'll talk a
little bit more about what the

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:39.011 align:middle line:84%
roles of these people are, but
having those roles defined

00:18:39.011 --> 00:18:42.940 align:middle line:90%
early is important.

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:45.530 align:middle line:84%
And then this is something to
talk about a bit, explaining

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:47.573 align:middle line:84%
the process and the principle
question at the

00:18:47.573 --> 00:18:49.930 align:middle line:90%
start of the session.

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.470 align:middle line:84%
How many of you had heard of
these four principles of

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:54.550 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming explicitly prior
to reading this text?

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.458 align:middle line:90%
Anybody?

00:18:56.458 --> 00:18:58.366 align:middle line:90%
Well.

00:18:58.366 --> 00:18:59.320 align:middle line:90%
I know.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:01.240 align:middle line:90%
You heard from me.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:05.940 align:middle line:84%
But the process is something
that needs to be clear.

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:07.490 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, people won't
remember to do it.

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:09.930 align:middle line:84%
You'll see when we do the
exercise, it's even hard

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:13.275 align:middle line:84%
sometimes to curb your
criticism, especially when

00:19:13.275 --> 00:19:13.890 align:middle line:84%
you're comfortable
with people.

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:16.380 align:middle line:84%
And you may not mean it in a
malicious way, or your may not

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:18.680 align:middle line:84%
mean it in a way to stop the
brainstorming session.

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:20.560 align:middle line:84%
But you'd just be like,
Oh, that's nuts.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:21.970 align:middle line:84%
You're laughing, and everybody
is having a good time.

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:24.690 align:middle line:84%
And then when you think back,
you're like, wait.

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:27.395 align:middle line:84%
That was me passing judgment
on an idea and possibly

00:19:27.395 --> 00:19:30.910 align:middle line:84%
inhibiting the kind of ideation
that we're trying to

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:31.340 align:middle line:90%
get across.

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:33.930 align:middle line:84%
So explaining the process at
the beginning regardless of

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.091 align:middle line:90%
the group is really important.

00:19:35.091 --> 00:19:36.500 align:middle line:84%
And that is usually a
responsibility that falls on

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.216 align:middle line:84%
the leader or the secretary,
whoever is

00:19:38.216 --> 00:19:39.802 align:middle line:90%
organizing the session.

00:19:39.802 --> 00:19:42.450 align:middle line:84%
They even suggest in the
text, have it posted.

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:45.320 align:middle line:84%
Have the four principles up so
that everybody can remind

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.160 align:middle line:84%
themselves through the
process what these

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.460 align:middle line:90%
four principles are.

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:50.950 align:middle line:84%
But explaining the process
is really important.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:55.210 align:middle line:90%
And then this is so important.

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:57.750 align:middle line:84%
Having the principal question
and the goal of the

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:01.290 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming session
clear at the start.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:04.870 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, it's encouraging
freewheeling.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:06.290 align:middle line:90%
It's encouraging free thought.

00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:08.515 align:middle line:84%
It's encouraging all kinds
of wide open creativity.

00:20:08.515 --> 00:20:11.740 align:middle line:84%
And at the same time, if you do
that in an unbounded sort

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:15.035 align:middle line:84%
of way, you might end up with
things that really in the end,

00:20:15.035 --> 00:20:17.480 align:middle line:84%
you don't have too many
useful ideas.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.925 align:middle line:84%
You have useful ideas for
something else, not for the

00:20:19.925 --> 00:20:22.370 align:middle line:84%
actually creative problem
that you have.

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So if you take it from an ad
concept, you have a vodka

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:29.270 align:middle line:84%
company that's like, oh, we need
an advertisement for our

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:30.390 align:middle line:90%
awesome vodka or whatever.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:31.540 align:middle line:90%
And so you're like, all right.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:32.190 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have a
brainstorm session.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:33.430 align:middle line:84%
And a bunch of people
come up with ideas.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:36.470 align:middle line:84%
Now you're selling
dryers because it

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:37.420 align:middle line:90%
was wide open, right?

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:40.465 align:middle line:84%
It's like, oh, we've got these
great ad ideas for dryers, but

00:20:40.465 --> 00:20:42.648 align:middle line:84%
they're just really not going
to work for vodka.

00:20:42.648 --> 00:20:46.150 align:middle line:84%
Bringing it back to the video
game contract, you've got a

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:48.520 align:middle line:84%
contract with a producer to make
a first person shooter,

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.000 align:middle line:84%
and in your brainstorming
session, you've designed a

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.360 align:middle line:90%
cool new platform.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:55.500 align:middle line:84%
Neat, but that's not where the
money is coming from, right?

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:59.135 align:middle line:84%
So bounding the brainstorm
session is important too.

00:20:59.135 --> 00:21:01.500 align:middle line:84%
And you'll see that in the
exercises that we do today,

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:02.930 align:middle line:84%
how it's important
to bound that.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:09.264 align:middle line:90%


00:21:09.264 --> 00:21:10.791 align:middle line:90%
Oh, and write down everything.

00:21:10.791 --> 00:21:13.100 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, I forgot about that.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.650 align:middle line:90%
This is an important point.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:17.280 align:middle line:90%


00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:19.347 align:middle line:84%
This happens to all of us, but
you have this great idea, and

00:21:19.347 --> 00:21:20.525 align:middle line:90%
then you don't write it down.

00:21:20.525 --> 00:21:21.942 align:middle line:84%
And then you go to bed, and
then the next day, you're

00:21:21.942 --> 00:21:24.570 align:middle line:84%
like, oh, I had this great
idea and you can't

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:25.280 align:middle line:90%
remember it, right?

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:29.750 align:middle line:84%
So writing down everything
is absolutely essential.

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:32.120 align:middle line:84%
You'd be amazed the number of
times that a week later,

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:35.790 align:middle line:84%
you're like, oh, we could put
these two ideas together as

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:37.960 align:middle line:84%
long as you can remember
what those ideas were.

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:41.550 align:middle line:84%
And so what you think, it's
hard to not mentally pass

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.310 align:middle line:84%
judgment on things during the
brainstorm session, and what

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:45.380 align:middle line:84%
you think might be great
now, may be a week

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:46.535 align:middle line:90%
later not be as great.

00:21:46.535 --> 00:21:48.490 align:middle line:84%
And what you didn't
think will be.

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:52.062 align:middle line:84%
And so making sure to have
everything written down is

00:21:52.062 --> 00:21:53.050 align:middle line:90%
really important.

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:56.035 align:middle line:84%
And they even suggest having two
secretaries if you need it

00:21:56.035 --> 00:21:59.390 align:middle line:84%
if you have a large group with
lot of ideas and they're each

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:00.630 align:middle line:90%
writing every other one.

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:03.050 align:middle line:84%
That can be helpful to make sure
that you get everything.

00:22:03.050 --> 00:22:07.900 align:middle line:90%


00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:10.930 align:middle line:84%
Next is the question of what
sort of problems are suitable

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:12.120 align:middle line:90%
to a brainstorming session.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:15.400 align:middle line:84%
The great example that Alex
Osborn gives is setting up a

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:19.200 align:middle line:84%
brainstorm session on whether or
not to get married is not a

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:20.940 align:middle line:84%
particularly suitable
question.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:23.370 align:middle line:84%
And that was an interesting
concept.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:26.460 align:middle line:84%
He's like, well, to decide to
get married, you apparently

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:28.285 align:middle line:90%
list the pros and cons.

00:22:28.285 --> 00:22:30.185 align:middle line:84%
That's not exactly the process
I went through

00:22:30.185 --> 00:22:31.135 align:middle line:90%
before I got married.

00:22:31.135 --> 00:22:32.321 align:middle line:84%
I was like, well honey,
I'm going to have

00:22:32.321 --> 00:22:33.571 align:middle line:90%
to write this down.

00:22:33.571 --> 00:22:35.410 align:middle line:90%


00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:38.290 align:middle line:84%
But, fair or not, that was the
suggestion that he came up.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:39.460 align:middle line:84%
And he had a good
point, right?

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:41.065 align:middle line:84%
The question of whether
or not to get married

00:22:41.065 --> 00:22:42.720 align:middle line:90%
is a judgment call.

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:45.370 align:middle line:84%
It is not a creative
ideation process.

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:49.860 align:middle line:84%
And so that's not a particularly
useful problem

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:54.170 align:middle line:84%
that's suitable to the
brainstorming process.

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:57.820 align:middle line:84%
You want a problem that is
going to be benefited by

00:22:57.820 --> 00:23:02.060 align:middle line:84%
having a large, selection,
a large corpus of ideas.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:04.050 align:middle line:84%
And so those are the kind of
problems that are more useful.

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:07.930 align:middle line:84%
And you'll see when we do our
example exercise what some of

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:08.900 align:middle line:90%
those might be.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:14.300 align:middle line:84%
I also put here that having a
clear definition of the aim is

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:14.690 align:middle line:90%
really essential.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:16.230 align:middle line:84%
It's what we talked
about before.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:18.450 align:middle line:84%
You need to make sure that
everybody understands and

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:19.200 align:middle line:90%
everybody's on the same page.

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:22.012 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, you'll end up with
stuff that might be useful for

00:23:22.012 --> 00:23:24.570 align:middle line:84%
something, but not useful for
what you're particularly

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:25.970 align:middle line:90%
trying to do.

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:29.980 align:middle line:84%
And so making that clear for
everybody is really important.

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:32.550 align:middle line:84%
Keeping the problem as
simple as possible

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:33.800 align:middle line:90%
is also really important.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:36.678 align:middle line:84%
Trying to keep simple language
for the problem.

00:23:36.678 --> 00:23:41.370 align:middle line:84%
Keep it constrained in a way
that is simple enough so that

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:45.270 align:middle line:84%
people can work on it as
opposed to just feeling

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:48.030 align:middle line:84%
limited by it, if that
makes any sense.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:50.240 align:middle line:84%
If something is overly
complicated and overly

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:52.860 align:middle line:84%
constrained, then it becomes,
well, how do I fit

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.260 align:middle line:90%
everything into this?

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:56.670 align:middle line:84%
How do I match every single
one of these things?

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:59.760 align:middle line:84%
If its' simpler, then it's a
little bit easier to work on

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:01.920 align:middle line:90%
and try to be creative with.

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:04.560 align:middle line:90%
So keeping problems simple.

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.310 align:middle line:84%
Which isn't to say that complex
problems couldn't be

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:10.010 align:middle line:84%
dealt with, but just divide
them into sub-problems.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:11.550 align:middle line:84%
Divide them into little
sections.

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:13.923 align:middle line:84%
So there might be something
like, how could we

00:24:13.923 --> 00:24:15.665 align:middle line:90%
deal with the UI?

00:24:15.665 --> 00:24:18.620 align:middle line:84%
How could it look
in this game?

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:20.720 align:middle line:84%
So rather than say, well how
are we going to design our

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:23.100 align:middle line:84%
first person shooter, you've
got a brainstorming session

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:25.600 align:middle line:84%
about what the UI could look
like for the first person

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:25.740 align:middle line:90%
shooting game.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:28.930 align:middle line:84%
You have maybe a brainstorming
session for what the fiction

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:30.400 align:middle line:84%
for this first person
shooter might be.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:32.935 align:middle line:84%
You're dividing it into
sub-problems rather than

00:24:32.935 --> 00:24:36.071 align:middle line:84%
having it be this gigantic
monolith of a thing.

00:24:36.071 --> 00:24:37.880 align:middle line:84%
And you can see how that would
be a problem, right?

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:39.760 align:middle line:84%
Because if you just brainstorm
on what's our first person

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:41.310 align:middle line:84%
shooter going to be,
you're going to get

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:42.430 align:middle line:90%
ideas about the UI.

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:43.590 align:middle line:84%
You're going to get ideas
about the fiction.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:45.459 align:middle line:84%
You're going to get ideas about
what sort of guns you

00:24:45.459 --> 00:24:47.580 align:middle line:84%
should use or what they
should sound like.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:50.925 align:middle line:84%
And it'll just be lots of
interesting ideas but

00:24:50.925 --> 00:24:52.175 align:middle line:90%
all over the place.

00:24:52.175 --> 00:24:54.240 align:middle line:90%


00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:58.284 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, the problem should
be clearly and simply

00:24:58.284 --> 00:24:58.768 align:middle line:90%
articulated.

00:24:58.768 --> 00:25:00.460 align:middle line:84%
I think that's what I said
by the shooting example.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:02.220 align:middle line:84%
Make sure that they're
articulated in a simple way.

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:04.486 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes you can have a simple
problem that's not

00:25:04.486 --> 00:25:06.260 align:middle line:84%
articulated particularly
clearly.

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:07.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's just something
to consider.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:11.110 align:middle line:90%


00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:18.150 align:middle line:84%
Responsibilities of the leader,
or whatever, the first

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:21.116 align:middle line:84%
being to understand and explain
the problem and the

00:25:21.116 --> 00:25:22.070 align:middle line:90%
aim of the brainstorm session.

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:24.650 align:middle line:84%
I mean a lot of times, people
maybe will write a memo

00:25:24.650 --> 00:25:26.200 align:middle line:90%
beforehand.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:29.140 align:middle line:84%
As the facilitator of the thing,
there's a person who

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:32.910 align:middle line:84%
perhaps wants to see the
brainstorm be a success.

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:36.710 align:middle line:84%
But mostly, having a clear
understanding of the problem.

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:39.040 align:middle line:84%
And explaining that to the group
is an important job for

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.122 align:middle line:90%
the leader.

00:25:40.122 --> 00:25:42.225 align:middle line:84%
I use this term facilitating
the session, encouraging

00:25:42.225 --> 00:25:44.230 align:middle line:84%
ideation, discouraging
criticism.

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:49.510 align:middle line:84%
You are the watch guard for the
process, not necessarily--

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:53.245 align:middle line:84%
you can sometimes be a
contributor and a watchdog for

00:25:53.245 --> 00:25:53.930 align:middle line:90%
the process.

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:56.558 align:middle line:84%
But most important
is that you are a

00:25:56.558 --> 00:25:59.250 align:middle line:90%
watchdog for the process.

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:03.042 align:middle line:84%
You'll see as we do it, it's
just so easy to slip into

00:26:03.042 --> 00:26:06.860 align:middle line:84%
these conversational habits
that are contrary to the

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.246 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming process.

00:26:08.246 --> 00:26:11.402 align:middle line:84%
And so having someone who's
making sure people don't

00:26:11.402 --> 00:26:13.080 align:middle line:84%
criticize and encouraging people
like oh, I haven't

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:13.660 align:middle line:90%
heard from this person.

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:14.941 align:middle line:90%
Do you have ideas?

00:26:14.941 --> 00:26:19.430 align:middle line:84%
Even pulling people
out is important.

00:26:19.430 --> 00:26:21.160 align:middle line:84%
And that comes down to ensuring
that no voices are

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:22.746 align:middle line:84%
lost during the discussion,
right?

00:26:22.746 --> 00:26:25.480 align:middle line:84%
I mean we had that ablest member
who had 15 ideas that

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.530 align:middle line:84%
he wrote down, and he's like,
they're worthless.

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:29.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, in some ways it's the
part of the leader, either

00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:31.910 align:middle line:84%
during the session or after
the session to try to

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:35.680 align:middle line:84%
encourage those able members
to contribute.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:39.072 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, some of the most crack
pot ideas become the best

00:26:39.072 --> 00:26:41.930 align:middle line:84%
ones, so you really want to make
sure ever voice is heard.

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:44.400 align:middle line:84%
And then encouraging the
synthesis, encouraging people

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:48.790 align:middle line:84%
to riff on other ideas, trying
to find ways for people to

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:49.900 align:middle line:90%
build on each other.

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:52.950 align:middle line:84%
Even offering synthesis towards
the directives.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:54.215 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you have
a couple ideas.

00:26:54.215 --> 00:26:57.250 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, oh, 1
and 10 are similar.

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:59.040 align:middle line:84%
Is there any way we could
combine this?

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:02.477 align:middle line:84%
And somebody is like, yeah, we
could give him a helmet.

00:27:02.477 --> 00:27:03.950 align:middle line:90%
Awesome.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:04.950 align:middle line:90%
So it's simple.

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:06.795 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what two
things you can

00:27:06.795 --> 00:27:08.120 align:middle line:90%
combine to get a helmet.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:09.520 align:middle line:90%
Can't get shot in the head.

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.600 align:middle line:90%
How about a helmet?

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:15.780 align:middle line:84%
All right, so responsibilities
for the secretary.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:17.290 align:middle line:84%
Most obviously, record
every and all ideas.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:18.712 align:middle line:90%
Write it all down.

00:27:18.712 --> 00:27:20.920 align:middle line:90%
You have to write it all down.

00:27:20.920 --> 00:27:22.240 align:middle line:90%
This is so important.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:25.546 align:middle line:84%
This one is often overlooked,
monitoring the schedule and

00:27:25.546 --> 00:27:28.270 align:middle line:90%
the duration of the session.

00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:31.070 align:middle line:84%
Something I learned a long,
long, long, time ago is one of

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.626 align:middle line:84%
the worst things that can happen
is meetings that run

00:27:33.626 --> 00:27:38.210 align:middle line:84%
over or meetings that start late
or meetings that don't

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:39.770 align:middle line:90%
adhere to a schedule.

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:43.910 align:middle line:84%
No better way to lose your
employees or lose your staff

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:47.850 align:middle line:90%
than to bite into their time.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:51.666 align:middle line:84%
And so making sure that it's
constrained and that it meets

00:27:51.666 --> 00:27:52.760 align:middle line:84%
the duration is really
important.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:56.260 align:middle line:84%
So, they seem simple, but
timing's a thing.

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:57.990 align:middle line:84%
And then of course, they look
like participating in the

00:27:57.990 --> 00:27:58.770 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:03.230 align:middle line:84%
Like I said, all voices should
be heard, and that oftentimes

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:06.960 align:middle line:84%
will mean that the secretary
is a creative

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:08.530 align:middle line:90%
professional as well.

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:13.300 align:middle line:84%
And so making sure that you
participate [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:18.927 align:middle line:84%
So we've got a few exercises now
to get you acclimated to

00:28:18.927 --> 00:28:21.120 align:middle line:90%
the idea of brainstorming.

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:23.890 align:middle line:84%
The first one is an actual
individual brainstorm.

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:26.446 align:middle line:84%
So if you have paper,
grab some paper at

00:28:26.446 --> 00:28:27.644 align:middle line:90%
the top of the pile.

00:28:27.644 --> 00:28:30.039 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If anyone is lacking
either writing

00:28:30.039 --> 00:28:31.476 align:middle line:90%
equipment or paper?

00:28:31.476 --> 00:28:33.871 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: You could use
a note card too.

00:28:33.871 --> 00:28:36.720 align:middle line:84%
Really just something to write
on other than the table.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:49.710 align:middle line:90%


00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:50.960 align:middle line:90%
Note cards are totally fine.

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:53.590 align:middle line:90%


00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:55.590 align:middle line:84%
As you guys are getting ready,
I'll continue to talk.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:57.724 align:middle line:84%
This is actually something that
I didn't mention in the

00:28:57.724 --> 00:28:59.605 align:middle line:84%
process but is actually
very useful to the

00:28:59.605 --> 00:29:00.640 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming process.

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:03.060 align:middle line:84%
I think doing warm ups
before you actually

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:04.250 align:middle line:90%
brainstorm is useful.

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:06.482 align:middle line:84%
You know how people show up just
from lunch, especially if

00:29:06.482 --> 00:29:09.472 align:middle line:84%
it's one of those
picnic meetings.

00:29:09.472 --> 00:29:11.360 align:middle line:90%
People sometimes are tired.

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:14.528 align:middle line:84%
People just might have other
things on the brain.

00:29:14.528 --> 00:29:17.653 align:middle line:84%
So getting into the habit,
getting warmed up to being

00:29:17.653 --> 00:29:21.080 align:middle line:90%
creative is important.

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:26.130 align:middle line:84%
I tend to find it's a rare kind
of person who you just

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:27.920 align:middle line:84%
give a blank piece of
paper to, and you're

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:29.030 align:middle line:90%
like, oh, make something.

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:29.895 align:middle line:90%
Go, be creative.

00:29:29.895 --> 00:29:31.715 align:middle line:90%
And they're like, vroom.

00:29:31.715 --> 00:29:33.080 align:middle line:90%
They just whip something out.

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:35.160 align:middle line:90%
You have to get warmed up.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.160 align:middle line:84%
Visual artists sketch
constantly,

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:38.360 align:middle line:90%
and that makes sense.

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:39.450 align:middle line:84%
And then they build
on sketches.

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:43.533 align:middle line:84%
I just make dumb pieces of music
that I'll maybe never

00:29:43.533 --> 00:29:45.132 align:middle line:84%
use for anything, but
maybe I will.

00:29:45.132 --> 00:29:45.840 align:middle line:90%
Who knows?

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:48.224 align:middle line:84%
I make stupid sound effects
in the shower.

00:29:48.224 --> 00:29:52.080 align:middle line:84%
I consider that warming
up for work actually.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:29:55.664 align:middle line:90%
So you have to get warmed up.

00:29:55.664 --> 00:29:58.508 align:middle line:84%
And so this is just a really
quick, five-minute warm up.

00:29:58.508 --> 00:30:01.060 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is, write anything
that comes to your

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:02.800 align:middle line:84%
mind for this specific
problem, and

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:03.270 align:middle line:90%
I'll show you a picture.

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:06.090 align:middle line:84%
Brainstorm possible uses-- it's
not really a brainstorm.

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:08.410 align:middle line:84%
It's more just that you
individually come up with

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:12.220 align:middle line:84%
creative ideas, creative uses,
for the following items.

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:15.090 align:middle line:84%
And I'll give you five
minutes starting now.

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:20.970 align:middle line:90%


00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:23.420 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:24.425 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully it was good
for a warm up.

00:30:24.425 --> 00:30:28.305 align:middle line:84%
Quick question by
a show of hand.

00:30:28.305 --> 00:30:29.275 align:middle line:90%
Be honest.

00:30:29.275 --> 00:30:32.185 align:middle line:90%
This is a safe environment.

00:30:32.185 --> 00:30:35.730 align:middle line:84%
How many of you had an idea that
you did not write down?

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:39.625 align:middle line:84%
How many of you self-censored
yourself with an idea?

00:30:39.625 --> 00:30:42.060 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, Tommy, you were like,
oh, that's dumb.

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:44.690 align:middle line:84%
See, it's hard to get out of
the habit of doing that.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:48.380 align:middle line:84%
It's hard to get out of this
habit of self-judgement.

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:51.600 align:middle line:90%
And it's really important.

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:53.580 align:middle line:84%
That dumb idea that you
think was worthless

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:54.760 align:middle line:90%
might be a good one.

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:57.260 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone who raised their
hand want to share what their

00:30:57.260 --> 00:31:00.236 align:middle line:84%
censored idea was just
to exorcise the

00:31:00.236 --> 00:31:03.866 align:middle line:90%
demons, so to speak?

00:31:03.866 --> 00:31:06.770 align:middle line:90%
No, no one?

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:08.667 align:middle line:84%
All right, who wants to
share some ideas that

00:31:08.667 --> 00:31:09.840 align:middle line:90%
they did write down?

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:11.440 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: See, I have a lot.

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:12.680 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, well
throw us a couple

00:31:12.680 --> 00:31:13.600 align:middle line:90%
that are your favorites.

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:17.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: C, lock pick,
MacGyver, connecting circuits.

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:17.870 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: There you go.

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:21.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Caring hurts, a
knife, clothes hanger,

00:31:21.680 --> 00:31:27.826 align:middle line:84%
chopsticks, dowser, stirrer,
connect batteries, let air out

00:31:27.826 --> 00:31:30.896 align:middle line:84%
of tires, emo tool,
nose picker.

00:31:30.896 --> 00:31:31.880 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Sure.

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:34.482 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Staple.

00:31:34.482 --> 00:31:35.790 align:middle line:90%
And a paper weight.

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:36.810 align:middle line:90%
I have a lot of things.

00:31:36.810 --> 00:31:38.594 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, good,
that's awesome.

00:31:38.594 --> 00:31:39.040 align:middle line:90%
Try it out?

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:42.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: All right, I got,
let's see, fingernail cleaner,

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:46.900 align:middle line:84%
an eyelash straightener,
basically rail gun ammo, a

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:49.750 align:middle line:84%
balloon annihilator,
and an official

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:51.560 align:middle line:90%
Tamagotchi TM resetter.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:55.570 align:middle line:90%


00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:57.080 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: It resets a lot
of things actually.

00:31:57.080 --> 00:31:58.010 align:middle line:90%
Over to this side.

00:31:58.010 --> 00:31:59.852 align:middle line:90%
Owen, go ahead.

00:31:59.852 --> 00:32:04.250 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I had [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:05.700 align:middle line:90%
I had scratch messaging tool.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:08.930 align:middle line:84%
I had used as a key ring,
conducted electricity with it.

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:11.550 align:middle line:90%
Made it into jewelry.

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:12.880 align:middle line:84%
Fixed umbrellas and what
have you with it,

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.285 align:middle line:90%
which I've done before.

00:32:15.285 --> 00:32:16.050 align:middle line:90%
A money clip.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:19.520 align:middle line:90%
Use it as a screw for cooking.

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:22.046 align:middle line:84%
Put it on top of the door, and
so when people open it and it

00:32:22.046 --> 00:32:24.008 align:middle line:84%
falls on the ground, you
know that somebody has

00:32:24.008 --> 00:32:25.258 align:middle line:90%
been in your room.

00:32:25.258 --> 00:32:28.320 align:middle line:90%


00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:30.060 align:middle line:90%
Lance boils, poke eyes out.

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:33.135 align:middle line:90%


00:32:33.135 --> 00:32:34.982 align:middle line:90%
Hair accessory.

00:32:34.982 --> 00:32:35.886 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:32:35.886 --> 00:32:37.360 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: That was
a good question.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:38.610 align:middle line:90%
Did you have a whole paper?

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:40.904 align:middle line:90%


00:32:40.904 --> 00:32:42.752 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:32:42.752 --> 00:32:46.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I had hairstyling
accessory, projectile,

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:51.830 align:middle line:84%
something you could melt down
for metal and other stuff.

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:52.616 align:middle line:84%
Something you could bend and
then put on chairs, and then

00:32:52.616 --> 00:32:53.866 align:middle line:84%
when people sit down,
it pokes them.

00:32:53.866 --> 00:32:56.150 align:middle line:90%


00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:58.210 align:middle line:90%
Modern art.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:01.980 align:middle line:84%
I thought you could get a bunch
and fill a dorm room

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.560 align:middle line:84%
with it as a prank
or something.

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:06.280 align:middle line:90%
Toothpick.

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:08.385 align:middle line:84%
Hold stuff open for
emergency surgery.

00:33:08.385 --> 00:33:12.618 align:middle line:90%


00:33:12.618 --> 00:33:13.104 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Cool.

00:33:13.104 --> 00:33:14.076 align:middle line:90%
Some other ideas?

00:33:14.076 --> 00:33:17.478 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I thought you could
bend it into an interesting

00:33:17.478 --> 00:33:20.880 align:middle line:84%
flat shape, and then put it in
ink and use it as a stamp.

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:22.350 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Very nice,
very good.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:24.524 align:middle line:90%
Cool, yeah, go ahead.

00:33:24.524 --> 00:33:26.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Game bits
and tokens.

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:29.990 align:middle line:84%
Holder for dipping something
into liquids.

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:34.319 align:middle line:84%
Tie clips and keeping small
boards together.

00:33:34.319 --> 00:33:34.800 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Very good.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:36.724 align:middle line:90%
Sure, yes?

00:33:36.724 --> 00:33:40.572 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A mini rock
garden rake.

00:33:40.572 --> 00:33:42.015 align:middle line:90%
Also a [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:42.015 --> 00:33:43.000 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: That is very good.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:43.620 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:45.140 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Heat generator.

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:48.322 align:middle line:84%
If you work hard on it by
bending it and unbending it,

00:33:48.322 --> 00:33:49.735 align:middle line:90%
then it gets hots.

00:33:49.735 --> 00:33:50.206 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Very nice.

00:33:50.206 --> 00:33:51.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: As long as
it doesn't break.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:51.445 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Yes?

00:33:51.445 --> 00:33:54.452 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think about a
souvenir as a bulletin for a

00:33:54.452 --> 00:33:55.926 align:middle line:90%
lost robot.

00:33:55.926 --> 00:33:58.341 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: That's good.

00:33:58.341 --> 00:33:59.950 align:middle line:90%
Say that again.

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:02.774 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say
that it's a robot that is made

00:34:02.774 --> 00:34:04.470 align:middle line:84%
by humans, but it's
lost in space.

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:07.338 align:middle line:84%
Then we have this thing that
reminded us of [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:07.338 --> 00:34:09.730 align:middle line:90%
a secret thing.

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:12.530 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: My favorite use for
a paper clip is cleaning the

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:15.730 align:middle line:84%
lint out of my iPhone ear
jack, which drives me

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:16.050 align:middle line:90%
absolutely crazy.

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:18.606 align:middle line:84%
So I'm running on reserve
battery power, and my slide

00:34:18.606 --> 00:34:20.798 align:middle line:90%
show has been suspended.

00:34:20.798 --> 00:34:27.130 align:middle line:84%
In my office, there's a air
powered connector thing.

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:29.210 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, how did we feel
about that exercise?

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:30.310 align:middle line:84%
It got you to loosen
up a little bit?

00:34:30.310 --> 00:34:31.820 align:middle line:84%
It got you thinking
about some ideas?

00:34:31.820 --> 00:34:35.070 align:middle line:84%
It got you feeling the creative
juices flowing?

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:38.370 align:middle line:84%
We have three more exercises
that we're going to do.

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:41.430 align:middle line:90%
So much brainstorming.

00:34:41.430 --> 00:34:43.730 align:middle line:84%
This is the hardest work
I've ever done at MIT.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:46.795 align:middle line:84%
No, so we have a few more
exercises to get you guys

00:34:46.795 --> 00:34:49.020 align:middle line:84%
comfortable, to get you
guys relaxed with this

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:50.418 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming process.

00:34:50.418 --> 00:34:52.282 align:middle line:84%
The next one is going to
be a group exercise.

00:34:52.282 --> 00:34:55.596 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to wait to describe
it until I can get

00:34:55.596 --> 00:34:56.989 align:middle line:90%
my slide show up.

00:34:56.989 --> 00:34:58.210 align:middle line:84%
Anybody else have
any particularly

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:00.990 align:middle line:90%
choice paper clip ideas?

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:02.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, I just
thought of one.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:03.300 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: What?

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:05.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A sine
wave generator.

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:06.860 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Oh, very good.

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:08.110 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wire frame doll.

00:35:08.110 --> 00:35:12.212 align:middle line:90%


00:35:12.212 --> 00:35:14.933 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] shaker.

00:35:14.933 --> 00:35:16.640 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Oh yeah, totally.

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:17.890 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:35:17.890 --> 00:35:25.110 align:middle line:90%


00:35:25.110 --> 00:35:26.595 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Hold up
a second guys.

00:35:26.595 --> 00:35:29.565 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If we stop looking at
it as an [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:35:29.565 --> 00:35:32.535 align:middle line:84%
Take it apart like an Army man
and use it as a spear.

00:35:32.535 --> 00:35:37.010 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, like Man Vs.
Wild catching fish with it.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:37.750 align:middle line:90%
Bear Grylls.

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:37.890 align:middle line:90%
Something

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:40.269 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: To chew on.

00:35:40.269 --> 00:35:42.738 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You could use it
as a catapult if you

00:35:42.738 --> 00:35:43.988 align:middle line:90%
bend the top part.

00:35:43.988 --> 00:35:46.126 align:middle line:90%


00:35:46.126 --> 00:35:47.376 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Rubber
band launcher.

00:35:47.376 --> 00:35:49.590 align:middle line:90%


00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:51.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Letting air
out of tires.

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:53.140 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:35:53.140 --> 00:36:04.242 align:middle line:90%


00:36:04.242 --> 00:36:05.492 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: There we go.

00:36:05.492 --> 00:36:09.280 align:middle line:90%


00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:09.760 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:11.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you remember that
guy who had an oversized red

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:16.040 align:middle line:84%
paper clip and ended up trading
it for a house?

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:17.290 align:middle line:90%
Currency towards a house.

00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:20.480 align:middle line:90%


00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:23.010 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Cool, so hopefully
that got you loosened up.

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:26.106 align:middle line:84%
I do want to just emphasize
again, for those of you who

00:36:26.106 --> 00:36:27.750 align:middle line:84%
raised your hand, and even
though of you who did not

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:29.830 align:middle line:84%
raise your hand because you
were censoring the truth

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:32.830 align:middle line:84%
because you were afraid,
try not to self-censor.

00:36:32.830 --> 00:36:34.330 align:middle line:84%
Really, it's important
to brainstorming.

00:36:34.330 --> 00:36:34.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It sounds
like the military.

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:37.330 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, stop
self-censoring.

00:36:37.330 --> 00:36:41.330 align:middle line:84%
I bet those crappy ideas that
you didn't write down are

00:36:41.330 --> 00:36:42.400 align:middle line:90%
actually really awesome.

00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:46.100 align:middle line:84%
So activity #2, we're going to
work as one large group.

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:47.300 align:middle line:90%
This is our conch shell.

00:36:47.300 --> 00:36:48.588 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't look like
one, I know.

00:36:48.588 --> 00:36:51.840 align:middle line:84%
But the principle
still applies.

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:56.010 align:middle line:84%
This is just to get us in the
habit, in the shape of

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:59.670 align:middle line:84%
speaking one at a time, not
interrupting each other.

00:36:59.670 --> 00:37:02.652 align:middle line:84%
And in some senses building on
each other's ideas too, right?

00:37:02.652 --> 00:37:05.360 align:middle line:84%
There's something in the
physical passing of the ball

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:08.080 align:middle line:84%
after you've heard someone's
idea that might encourage that

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:11.040 align:middle line:84%
sort of synthesis of ideas
as you're going.

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:13.566 align:middle line:84%
I will be your fearless
leader.

00:37:13.566 --> 00:37:15.641 align:middle line:84%
All that means is that I just
make sure you're not

00:37:15.641 --> 00:37:17.980 align:middle line:90%
interrupting each other.

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:23.230 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to brainstorm
solutions to a design problem.

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:25.666 align:middle line:84%
This one I think is actually
a lot of fun.

00:37:25.666 --> 00:37:27.390 align:middle line:84%
So, I will show you the
design problem.

00:37:27.390 --> 00:37:30.830 align:middle line:84%
I will maybe throw out an idea,
and then I will toss the

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:33.190 align:middle line:84%
conch shell to the first
person who wants it.

00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:34.570 align:middle line:84%
And then it doesn't have
to go back to me.

00:37:34.570 --> 00:37:36.730 align:middle line:84%
Just anyone who wants it,
throw up your hand.

00:37:36.730 --> 00:37:38.630 align:middle line:90%
