01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:27.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: All right guys.

01:03:27.530 --> 01:03:28.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Our publisher
is going to

01:03:28.290 --> 01:03:29.540 align:middle line:90%
give us so much money.

01:03:29.540 --> 01:03:33.890 align:middle line:90%


01:03:33.890 --> 01:03:36.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It may seem odd to
have you do this brainstorm,

01:03:36.640 --> 01:03:40.180 align:middle line:84%
and then what I actually want
to talk about is rather than

01:03:40.180 --> 01:03:41.566 align:middle line:84%
give [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
because I'm sure

01:03:41.566 --> 01:03:42.028 align:middle line:90%
they're all really good.

01:03:42.028 --> 01:03:43.414 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Ahh.

01:03:43.414 --> 01:03:44.230 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: I know, so sad.

01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:47.980 align:middle line:84%
But what really matters is the
process, because the point of

01:03:47.980 --> 01:03:50.200 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming is to
withhold judgment.

01:03:50.200 --> 01:03:54.817 align:middle line:84%
Judging which of your ideas is
best shouldn't have even been

01:03:54.817 --> 01:03:55.862 align:middle line:90%
happening at this stage.

01:03:55.862 --> 01:03:58.240 align:middle line:90%
And so one kind of question--

01:03:58.240 --> 01:04:00.140 align:middle line:84%
we'll come back to
what went well--

01:04:00.140 --> 01:04:05.360 align:middle line:84%
but what sort of stumbling
blocks did you guys run into

01:04:05.360 --> 01:04:06.740 align:middle line:84%
in this process as you
were doing it?

01:04:06.740 --> 01:04:07.770 align:middle line:90%
Did it all go perfectly?

01:04:07.770 --> 01:04:10.265 align:middle line:90%
I highly doubt it.

01:04:10.265 --> 01:04:13.956 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I would say we were
trying to design this whole

01:04:13.956 --> 01:04:15.255 align:middle line:84%
game, so sometimes we
would say features.

01:04:15.255 --> 01:04:16.752 align:middle line:90%
And then we [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:16.752 --> 01:04:23.239 align:middle line:90%


01:04:23.239 --> 01:04:25.334 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, in some senses,
it may have been

01:04:25.334 --> 01:04:25.734 align:middle line:90%
constrained in one area.

01:04:25.734 --> 01:04:28.230 align:middle line:84%
Sure, zombies and
a love story.

01:04:28.230 --> 01:04:30.962 align:middle line:84%
But then it was asking for a
whole lot, and so it could

01:04:30.962 --> 01:04:33.208 align:middle line:84%
have been, can we just
brainstorm a title.

01:04:33.208 --> 01:04:35.650 align:middle line:84%
Or could we just brainstorm
this or brainstorm that.

01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:36.050 align:middle line:90%
What about you?

01:04:36.050 --> 01:04:38.972 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We very briefly got
sidetracked on Pokemon.

01:04:38.972 --> 01:04:41.520 align:middle line:90%


01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:45.050 align:middle line:84%
For a second, it was Pokemon
only, Pokemon

01:04:45.050 --> 01:04:46.300 align:middle line:90%
first person shooter.

01:04:46.300 --> 01:04:50.070 align:middle line:90%


01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:51.210 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Any other thoughts?

01:04:51.210 --> 01:04:52.440 align:middle line:84%
I mean you can talk about
the whole group.

01:04:52.440 --> 01:04:54.270 align:middle line:84%
You can talk about your own
difficulty you had personally,

01:04:54.270 --> 01:04:58.585 align:middle line:84%
whether you had any
challenges with--

01:04:58.585 --> 01:05:00.851 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I feel like if
somebody says a really good

01:05:00.851 --> 01:05:03.190 align:middle line:84%
idea, then everybody is stuck on
that idea. and you have to

01:05:03.190 --> 01:05:05.070 align:middle line:84%
get through that whole
idea before any other

01:05:05.070 --> 01:05:05.960 align:middle line:90%
ideas can come out.

01:05:05.960 --> 01:05:09.156 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, that's true.

01:05:09.156 --> 01:05:09.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We had a
stumbling block.

01:05:09.650 --> 01:05:14.590 align:middle line:84%
What other MMOs are
there out there?

01:05:14.590 --> 01:05:17.554 align:middle line:84%
So [INAUDIBLE] a
few other ones.

01:05:17.554 --> 01:05:21.012 align:middle line:84%
That was a dead spot in
the brainstorming.

01:05:21.012 --> 01:05:22.494 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Sure.

01:05:22.494 --> 01:05:23.976 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I was the scribe.

01:05:23.976 --> 01:05:28.422 align:middle line:84%
It was hard to jump in and
talk about your own ideas

01:05:28.422 --> 01:05:29.800 align:middle line:84%
because I was so busy writing
everyone else's.

01:05:29.800 --> 01:05:31.250 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: It was really hard
with brainstorming.

01:05:31.250 --> 01:05:33.316 align:middle line:84%
You guys actually-- both
groups-- but I

01:05:33.316 --> 01:05:34.615 align:middle line:90%
stopped in with you guys.

01:05:34.615 --> 01:05:36.001 align:middle line:84%
You guys had this interesting
momentum going, and I was

01:05:36.001 --> 01:05:37.920 align:middle line:84%
watching you try to
write it all down.

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:41.340 align:middle line:84%
It was way hard because the
ideas were coming in as you

01:05:41.340 --> 01:05:42.560 align:middle line:90%
were finishing the last idea.

01:05:42.560 --> 01:05:47.530 align:middle line:84%
So if a group has really good
momentum, I actually really

01:05:47.530 --> 01:05:50.752 align:middle line:84%
like the idea of having two
people where each person is

01:05:50.752 --> 01:05:51.110 align:middle line:90%
writing every other one.

01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:54.210 align:middle line:84%
Of course the difficulty is then
you pull two people out

01:05:54.210 --> 01:05:54.810 align:middle line:90%
of contributing.

01:05:54.810 --> 01:05:59.164 align:middle line:84%
So the organization of the
actual group is important.

01:05:59.164 --> 01:06:00.788 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Do you ever recommend

01:06:00.788 --> 01:06:01.950 align:middle line:90%
using a tape recorder?

01:06:01.950 --> 01:06:02.600 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Yeah.

01:06:02.600 --> 01:06:04.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, absolutely.

01:06:04.100 --> 01:06:05.380 align:middle line:84%
I mean it certainly
makes sense.

01:06:05.380 --> 01:06:08.924 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that Osborn
says which I think is--

01:06:08.924 --> 01:06:10.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can't put
a tape recorder down.

01:06:10.780 --> 01:06:11.244 align:middle line:90%
I remember that.

01:06:11.244 --> 01:06:13.670 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, but he also
says that one of the most

01:06:13.670 --> 01:06:16.290 align:middle line:84%
important things for the
secretary scribe is to number

01:06:16.290 --> 01:06:20.440 align:middle line:84%
things so that you know
how many you have.

01:06:20.440 --> 01:06:23.651 align:middle line:84%
Is that just to support
your work?

01:06:23.651 --> 01:06:26.453 align:middle line:84%
Look, we came up
with 110 ideas.

01:06:26.453 --> 01:06:27.854 align:middle line:90%
Amazing.

01:06:27.854 --> 01:06:30.090 align:middle line:84%
How many doesn't seem to
matter as much as the

01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:30.540 align:middle line:90%
qualities of the ideas.

01:06:30.540 --> 01:06:33.216 align:middle line:84%
So I thought it was weird
that he said it.

01:06:33.216 --> 01:06:36.280 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, tape recording,
video recording.

01:06:36.280 --> 01:06:38.393 align:middle line:84%
Any way of documenting the
process is I think the most

01:06:38.393 --> 01:06:39.590 align:middle line:90%
important part.

01:06:39.590 --> 01:06:42.160 align:middle line:84%
And actually, he does make a
good point of saying that one

01:06:42.160 --> 01:06:45.066 align:middle line:84%
of the problems with
brainstorming as it evolved

01:06:45.066 --> 01:06:47.667 align:middle line:84%
from when he first introduced it
at this one place and then

01:06:47.667 --> 01:06:49.800 align:middle line:84%
people started running with it
without thinking about the

01:06:49.800 --> 01:06:52.250 align:middle line:84%
principles is they would
brainstorm, and then that was

01:06:52.250 --> 01:06:52.870 align:middle line:90%
sort of it.

01:06:52.870 --> 01:06:56.240 align:middle line:84%
And you really have to
look back at what

01:06:56.240 --> 01:06:57.820 align:middle line:90%
you had come up with.

01:06:57.820 --> 01:07:00.476 align:middle line:84%
It's really important to reflect
on what you've done as

01:07:00.476 --> 01:07:02.300 align:middle line:90%
a second step.

01:07:02.300 --> 01:07:04.750 align:middle line:90%
And so that's important too.

01:07:04.750 --> 01:07:08.010 align:middle line:90%
How about the judgement thing?

01:07:08.010 --> 01:07:10.020 align:middle line:84%
I didn't notice a whole
lot of criticism.

01:07:10.020 --> 01:07:10.370 align:middle line:90%
It was really good.

01:07:10.370 --> 01:07:12.560 align:middle line:84%
Nobody was like, that's
a terrible idea.

01:07:12.560 --> 01:07:15.270 align:middle line:84%
But did you find it hard to not
pass judgment on ideas as

01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:16.500 align:middle line:90%
they were coming out?

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:18.468 align:middle line:90%
Even your idea?

01:07:18.468 --> 01:07:21.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I mean I
definitely let more than one,

01:07:21.980 --> 01:07:24.590 align:middle line:90%
"I like that" slips.

01:07:24.590 --> 01:07:27.350 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: That's OK.

01:07:27.350 --> 01:07:30.100 align:middle line:84%
What you want to avoid is like
the, oh my god, we've got to

01:07:30.100 --> 01:07:31.620 align:middle line:90%
do this one.

01:07:31.620 --> 01:07:35.868 align:middle line:84%
That will derail everything
because then it's just a loud

01:07:35.868 --> 01:07:37.644 align:middle line:84%
voice that proclaims
that we're done

01:07:37.644 --> 01:07:40.010 align:middle line:90%
because we got the idea.

01:07:40.010 --> 01:07:43.180 align:middle line:84%
But nothing encourages a person
who is shy more than

01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:45.250 align:middle line:84%
feeling like their idea
actually was good or

01:07:45.250 --> 01:07:45.734 align:middle line:90%
meritorious.

01:07:45.734 --> 01:07:49.620 align:middle line:84%
And so that sort of
stuff is fine.

01:07:49.620 --> 01:07:53.170 align:middle line:84%
You want to avoid the
stop the process.

01:07:53.170 --> 01:07:56.095 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think I did say at
some point, ah, we've got our

01:07:56.095 --> 01:07:57.330 align:middle line:84%
title, which is probably
not good.

01:07:57.330 --> 01:07:59.628 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, it's
just a hard thing to

01:07:59.628 --> 01:08:01.350 align:middle line:90%
train yourself to do.

01:08:01.350 --> 01:08:02.334 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is it Necrophilia?

01:08:02.334 --> 01:08:03.810 align:middle line:90%
A great title.

01:08:03.810 --> 01:08:05.800 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Are there
self-censors still out there?

01:08:05.800 --> 01:08:08.244 align:middle line:84%
Were there a lot of people who
thought of ideas but then just

01:08:08.244 --> 01:08:09.218 align:middle line:90%
didn't contribute?

01:08:09.218 --> 01:08:11.166 align:middle line:90%
Good, you didn't.

01:08:11.166 --> 01:08:15.498 align:middle line:84%
It's great if everybody
felt free.

01:08:15.498 --> 01:08:18.880 align:middle line:90%
Cool, that's really good.

01:08:18.880 --> 01:08:21.160 align:middle line:84%
So I would say that's one
thing that went well.

01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:23.649 align:middle line:84%
What other things went
well for you guys?

01:08:23.649 --> 01:08:25.870 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The tagline.

01:08:25.870 --> 01:08:29.488 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Specifically, just
coming up with taglines?

01:08:29.488 --> 01:08:32.149 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Infected by the--

01:08:32.149 --> 01:08:34.136 align:middle line:90%
Is it love after life.

01:08:34.136 --> 01:08:34.870 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: That's cool.

01:08:34.870 --> 01:08:36.399 align:middle line:84%
Did you guys feel like
you had momentum?

01:08:36.399 --> 01:08:40.240 align:middle line:84%
It seemed to me watching that
there was momentum building.

01:08:40.240 --> 01:08:42.910 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think we did a
really good job of taking a

01:08:42.910 --> 01:08:46.750 align:middle line:84%
lot of different ideas that we
had and synthesizing into

01:08:46.750 --> 01:08:47.029 align:middle line:90%
different things.

01:08:47.029 --> 01:08:48.390 align:middle line:84%
Saying, oh, that idea would
work well if we did this.

01:08:48.390 --> 01:08:50.306 align:middle line:84%
Oh, and then we could
use that name, and

01:08:50.306 --> 01:08:51.384 align:middle line:90%
that would fit there.

01:08:51.384 --> 01:08:51.815 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Yeah.

01:08:51.815 --> 01:08:57.010 align:middle line:84%
Who were the two leaders for
the two groups, the clerks?

01:08:57.010 --> 01:09:00.080 align:middle line:84%
Did you guys feel like you had
to do much facilitating.

01:09:00.080 --> 01:09:01.399 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Not really, the hard
part was really at the

01:09:01.399 --> 01:09:03.489 align:middle line:84%
beginning, where it was,
how do we want to

01:09:03.489 --> 01:09:04.282 align:middle line:90%
approach this thing?

01:09:04.282 --> 01:09:06.819 align:middle line:84%
And so I guess we decided
to start with the title.

01:09:06.819 --> 01:09:10.113 align:middle line:84%
It was either the titles
or the main features.

01:09:10.113 --> 01:09:12.427 align:middle line:84%
And we were just like, let's
come up with a whole bunch of

01:09:12.427 --> 01:09:14.041 align:middle line:90%
titles and go from there.

01:09:14.041 --> 01:09:15.514 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Sure.

01:09:15.514 --> 01:09:18.479 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I forgot
I was the leader.

01:09:18.479 --> 01:09:21.420 align:middle line:90%
I didn't lead anything.

01:09:21.420 --> 01:09:23.893 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: If you don't have a
whole lot of facilitating to

01:09:23.893 --> 01:09:26.679 align:middle line:84%
do, it generally means that
the group is running OK.

01:09:26.679 --> 01:09:28.160 align:middle line:84%
I do think you have
a good point.

01:09:28.160 --> 01:09:30.943 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the facilitator's work
comes at the beginning of

01:09:30.943 --> 01:09:33.720 align:middle line:84%
the process, or even before the
process began, making sure

01:09:33.720 --> 01:09:35.190 align:middle line:90%
the constraints are clear.

01:09:35.190 --> 01:09:37.476 align:middle line:84%
And then you guys know
what you are going

01:09:37.476 --> 01:09:39.110 align:middle line:90%
to be working on.

01:09:39.110 --> 01:09:41.780 align:middle line:84%
OK, so bringing this back--
this is a game

01:09:41.780 --> 01:09:42.020 align:middle line:90%
design class, right?--

01:09:42.020 --> 01:09:43.760 align:middle line:90%
I touched on it earlier.

01:09:43.760 --> 01:09:48.715 align:middle line:84%
How do you guys see this as
being a useful mechanic or a

01:09:48.715 --> 01:09:50.560 align:middle line:90%
useful tool You know what?

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:51.670 align:middle line:84%
It's funny I called
it a mechanic.

01:09:51.670 --> 01:09:53.674 align:middle line:84%
Did any of you catch that
paragraph in Alex Osborn where

01:09:53.674 --> 01:09:56.080 align:middle line:84%
the guy was saying, "When my
team feels like they're

01:09:56.080 --> 01:09:58.970 align:middle line:84%
playing, it's a way more
productive brainstorm than

01:09:58.970 --> 01:10:01.840 align:middle line:84%
when not." And I thought that
was really interesting because

01:10:01.840 --> 01:10:06.095 align:middle line:84%
there are elements of gaminess
to this, which I think is

01:10:06.095 --> 01:10:08.080 align:middle line:90%
interesting.

01:10:08.080 --> 01:10:12.069 align:middle line:84%
So how do you guys this could
improve the general game

01:10:12.069 --> 01:10:13.319 align:middle line:90%
design process?

01:10:13.319 --> 01:10:16.542 align:middle line:90%


01:10:16.542 --> 01:10:19.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like after
you have a game and you're

01:10:19.530 --> 01:10:23.340 align:middle line:84%
testing it, and there's a major
flaw that is taking too

01:10:23.340 --> 01:10:24.184 align:middle line:90%
long or something like that.

01:10:24.184 --> 01:10:27.148 align:middle line:84%
And everyone sits down in a
brainstorm and says, this is

01:10:27.148 --> 01:10:28.630 align:middle line:90%
exactly what our problem is.

01:10:28.630 --> 01:10:30.112 align:middle line:90%
How can we fix it?

01:10:30.112 --> 01:10:33.080 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, overcoming
road blocks.

01:10:33.080 --> 01:10:35.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We never would have
come up with half the cool

01:10:35.230 --> 01:10:36.565 align:middle line:84%
ideas that we came up
during the process.

01:10:36.565 --> 01:10:38.940 align:middle line:84%
Just me thinking alone never
would have come up with

01:10:38.940 --> 01:10:39.655 align:middle line:90%
Pokemon zombies.

01:10:39.655 --> 01:10:42.156 align:middle line:90%
ABE STEIN: Sure, anyone else?

01:10:42.156 --> 01:10:45.440 align:middle line:90%


01:10:45.440 --> 01:10:48.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, this is good
just in general just

01:10:48.430 --> 01:10:49.340 align:middle line:90%
to think up a game.

01:10:49.340 --> 01:10:52.830 align:middle line:84%
Because maybe it's like,
oh, every single

01:10:52.830 --> 01:10:53.720 align:middle line:90%
game has been decided.

01:10:53.720 --> 01:10:55.360 align:middle line:84%
People make games
of all types.

01:10:55.360 --> 01:10:57.400 align:middle line:84%
How am I going to make
a unique game.

01:10:57.400 --> 01:10:59.440 align:middle line:84%
This brainstorm is like,
hmm, those two things.

01:10:59.440 --> 01:11:00.810 align:middle line:90%
I've never seen that before.

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:01.670 align:middle line:90%
That could be interesting.

01:11:01.670 --> 01:11:02.640 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and
that's a really

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:06.204 align:middle line:90%
important good point actually.

01:11:06.204 --> 01:11:09.662 align:middle line:84%
The synthesis thing is actually
I think something

01:11:09.662 --> 01:11:11.885 align:middle line:84%
that is really important because
we tend to see a lot

01:11:11.885 --> 01:11:13.580 align:middle line:90%
of recycled mechanics.

01:11:13.580 --> 01:11:15.840 align:middle line:84%
And what becomes the interesting
thing in a game

01:11:15.840 --> 01:11:19.590 align:middle line:84%
oftentimes is not that it's a
completely new rule, right?

01:11:19.590 --> 01:11:22.180 align:middle line:84%
It's the way that rule interacts
with the other rules

01:11:22.180 --> 01:11:23.470 align:middle line:90%
in the system.

01:11:23.470 --> 01:11:26.530 align:middle line:84%
And in some instances, it's also
how that rule is read and

01:11:26.530 --> 01:11:29.442 align:middle line:84%
interpreted by the player, or
perhaps tends to be better in

01:11:29.442 --> 01:11:30.630 align:middle line:90%
terms of other players.

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:33.955 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, I think the synthesis
is actually an interesting

01:11:33.955 --> 01:11:36.833 align:middle line:84%
part of this where people can
bring a lot of different ideas

01:11:36.833 --> 01:11:37.910 align:middle line:84%
from a lot of different
experiences with

01:11:37.910 --> 01:11:40.555 align:middle line:90%
games to fuse ideas.

01:11:40.555 --> 01:11:45.405 align:middle line:90%


01:11:45.405 --> 01:11:47.745 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When it comes to
coming up with ideas for

01:11:47.745 --> 01:11:49.122 align:middle line:84%
games, the nice thing about
brainstorming is that it gets

01:11:49.122 --> 01:11:51.710 align:middle line:84%
past the good idea that everyone
happens to make,

01:11:51.710 --> 01:11:56.946 align:middle line:84%
which is the problem that we
sometimes have in the classes.

01:11:56.946 --> 01:12:00.418 align:middle line:84%
The people will find one idea
that they like, and they will

01:12:00.418 --> 01:12:04.386 align:middle line:84%
never think past it because they
were happy to find one

01:12:04.386 --> 01:12:04.900 align:middle line:90%
that everybody agreed with.

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:07.230 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and so you guys
are divided into design

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:08.480 align:middle line:84%
teams, and one of the things
I would encourage--

01:12:08.480 --> 01:12:12.210 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how it's set up
schedule-wise, but try to

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:16.304 align:middle line:84%
divide the brainstorming process
from the judgment,

01:12:16.304 --> 01:12:17.690 align:middle line:90%
like I said.

01:12:17.690 --> 01:12:19.080 align:middle line:90%
It's true.

01:12:19.080 --> 01:12:22.580 align:middle line:84%
Even if you do the brainstorm,
it can help you get over.

01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:24.540 align:middle line:84%
But a lot of times, whatever
had momentum during that

01:12:24.540 --> 01:12:26.450 align:middle line:84%
brainstorm or whatever got
the most yuks during that

01:12:26.450 --> 01:12:29.930 align:middle line:84%
brainstorm is going to be the
popular, strong idea.

01:12:29.930 --> 01:12:31.813 align:middle line:84%
And then when you come back to
it with a little space, you

01:12:31.813 --> 01:12:34.808 align:middle line:84%
might be like, oh, that it
is a really good idea.

01:12:34.808 --> 01:12:37.538 align:middle line:84%
But there was this other thing
that got mentioned half an

01:12:37.538 --> 01:12:38.154 align:middle line:84%
hour earlier that's really
interesting.

01:12:38.154 --> 01:12:40.066 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we can incorporate
that somehow.

01:12:40.066 --> 01:12:44.812 align:middle line:84%
And so making the judgment
happen in a separate phase can

01:12:44.812 --> 01:12:46.230 align:middle line:90%
be really important.

01:12:46.230 --> 01:12:49.220 align:middle line:84%
Because it's true, creativity is
a little bit more organic.

01:12:49.220 --> 01:12:51.180 align:middle line:90%
It's not necessarily a strictly

01:12:51.180 --> 01:12:52.970 align:middle line:90%
logical process, right?

01:12:52.970 --> 01:12:57.714 align:middle line:84%
So applying logic later can be
a good thing, I think, to the

01:12:57.714 --> 01:13:00.210 align:middle line:90%
creative process.

01:13:00.210 --> 01:13:03.120 align:middle line:84%
Cool, any questions about
brainstorming at all?

01:13:03.120 --> 01:13:07.700 align:middle line:84%
You are incredibly enlightened
by Alex Osborn?

01:13:07.700 --> 01:13:08.950 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:08.950 --> 01:13:12.110 align:middle line:90%


01:13:12.110 --> 01:13:16.275 align:middle line:84%
using things like Google Wave
or Google Docs where things

01:13:16.275 --> 01:13:17.745 align:middle line:84%
are automatically recorded
because everyone is basically

01:13:17.745 --> 01:13:19.960 align:middle line:84%
typing in their ideas
as it's happening.

01:13:19.960 --> 01:13:21.970 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and I think
it's interesting.

01:13:21.970 --> 01:13:24.654 align:middle line:84%
I don't have a whole lot
of experience with--

01:13:24.654 --> 01:13:27.810 align:middle line:90%
well, I should rephrase.

01:13:27.810 --> 01:13:30.280 align:middle line:84%
I don't have a lot of experience
with remote

01:13:30.280 --> 01:13:35.420 align:middle line:84%
brainstorming, but I one of
the games I worked on this

01:13:35.420 --> 01:13:37.270 align:middle line:84%
summer was this game called
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:13:37.270 --> 01:13:40.590 align:middle line:84%
where you were asked a lot
of personal questions.

01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:43.110 align:middle line:84%
And so we had to think of
personal questions.

01:13:43.110 --> 01:13:45.275 align:middle line:84%
So we were like, well, if you
have ideas, write them down in

01:13:45.275 --> 01:13:45.720 align:middle line:90%
Google Docs.

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:48.610 align:middle line:84%
And at one point, myself and
two of the other designers

01:13:48.610 --> 01:13:49.680 align:middle line:84%
were all on the Google
Doc at the same time.

01:13:49.680 --> 01:13:51.120 align:middle line:84%
And you could actually
see that that sort of

01:13:51.120 --> 01:13:52.860 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming would happen.

01:13:52.860 --> 01:13:54.106 align:middle line:84%
Someone would type
something in.

01:13:54.106 --> 01:13:55.026 align:middle line:84%
And then someone would type next
to it, ha ha, that's so

01:13:55.026 --> 01:13:58.870 align:middle line:84%
funny, and then write an idea
that's related to that.

01:13:58.870 --> 01:14:01.920 align:middle line:84%
And you're seeing it happen
in this digital space.

01:14:01.920 --> 01:14:05.690 align:middle line:84%
So I think we've gotten so
accustomed to having these

01:14:05.690 --> 01:14:10.336 align:middle line:84%
digital personalities or these
digital personas in IMing and

01:14:10.336 --> 01:14:13.682 align:middle line:84%
text messaging and all that
other stuff that I think we've

01:14:13.682 --> 01:14:15.180 align:middle line:84%
gotten pretty adept at typing
fast enough to get ideas out

01:14:15.180 --> 01:14:16.810 align:middle line:84%
that I think it could
totally work.

01:14:16.810 --> 01:14:19.642 align:middle line:84%
There's something to be said
for the momentum that gets

01:14:19.642 --> 01:14:21.106 align:middle line:90%
generated in person.

01:14:21.106 --> 01:14:21.960 align:middle line:90%
But yeah--

01:14:21.960 --> 01:14:22.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You could
try both ways.

01:14:22.950 --> 01:14:23.300 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, I mean
it's so funny.

01:14:23.300 --> 01:14:24.051 align:middle line:84%
I was going to say, no that's
a terrible idea.

01:14:24.051 --> 01:14:25.033 align:middle line:84%
You have to be around
each other.

01:14:25.033 --> 01:14:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, actually,
it works.

01:14:28.470 --> 01:14:29.287 align:middle line:84%
I think it all depends
a little bit on the

01:14:29.287 --> 01:14:32.400 align:middle line:84%
personalities of the people
that were involved.

01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:34.950 align:middle line:84%
Certainly for a prolonged
brainstorm--

01:14:34.950 --> 01:14:38.450 align:middle line:84%
one of the thing Alex Osborn
says is time box it.

01:14:38.450 --> 01:14:40.450 align:middle line:84%
If you don't time box it, it's
going to be a problem.

01:14:40.450 --> 01:14:42.990 align:middle line:84%
I think for in person,
that's true.

01:14:42.990 --> 01:14:47.320 align:middle line:84%
It's so easy to get onto a
Google document now whenever

01:14:47.320 --> 01:14:50.680 align:middle line:84%
you have an idea that actually
having a long form brainstorm

01:14:50.680 --> 01:14:52.690 align:middle line:84%
where anytime someone has an
idea over a week or something

01:14:52.690 --> 01:14:55.060 align:middle line:90%
like that, write it down.

01:14:55.060 --> 01:14:57.850 align:middle line:84%
That's also useful too because
then you separated the process

01:14:57.850 --> 01:15:01.290 align:middle line:90%
into collecting and--

01:15:01.290 --> 01:15:04.230 align:middle line:84%
But you still should do that,
building on ideas.

01:15:04.230 --> 01:15:07.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's probably easier
just to do this [INAUDIBLE]

01:15:07.810 --> 01:15:11.650 align:middle line:84%
when you're simultaneously
online or in the same room.

01:15:11.650 --> 01:15:14.626 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and to
see the ideas come out

01:15:14.626 --> 01:15:15.980 align:middle line:90%
as they come out.

01:15:15.980 --> 01:15:20.628 align:middle line:84%
Because it's a little uncommon
to just read something and be

01:15:20.628 --> 01:15:23.710 align:middle line:84%
like, oh yeah, what about this
when it first shows up.

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:27.141 align:middle line:84%
Or when it first comes into
existence as you're trying to

01:15:27.141 --> 01:15:30.001 align:middle line:84%
think of things that
can spur ideas.

01:15:30.001 --> 01:15:33.258 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The other danger about
doing it like, OK, this

01:15:33.258 --> 01:15:35.505 align:middle line:84%
document is open for the next 24
hours, everyone throw your

01:15:35.505 --> 01:15:38.106 align:middle line:84%
ideas in, is that then people
start spending too

01:15:38.106 --> 01:15:39.300 align:middle line:90%
long on any one idea.

01:15:39.300 --> 01:15:40.928 align:middle line:84%
They start thinking about what
they're going to write down

01:15:40.928 --> 01:15:42.184 align:middle line:90%
before they write it down.

01:15:42.184 --> 01:15:44.539 align:middle line:90%
No, that's not the point.

01:15:44.539 --> 01:15:46.700 align:middle line:84%
ABE STEIN: Yeah, think of the
paper clip experience, and

01:15:46.700 --> 01:15:48.790 align:middle line:84%
those of you that raised your
hand for saying that you were

01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:49.860 align:middle line:90%
self-censoring.

01:15:49.860 --> 01:15:51.450 align:middle line:90%
On your own--

01:15:51.450 --> 01:15:54.196 align:middle line:84%
I wasn't going to make you all
hand in those paper clip ideas

01:15:54.196 --> 01:15:55.005 align:middle line:90%
you were self-censoring.

01:15:55.005 --> 01:15:57.430 align:middle line:84%
So the same sort of
thing applies.

01:15:57.430 --> 01:16:01.800 align:middle line:84%
You might censor yourself or
spend a long time on ideas.

01:16:01.800 --> 01:16:04.566 align:middle line:90%
Any other thoughts, questions?

01:16:04.566 --> 01:16:06.425 align:middle line:84%
Cool, well I hope this
was helpful.

01:16:06.425 --> 01:16:09.185 align:middle line:84%
I hope it'll be helpful for
your game design stuff.

01:16:09.185 --> 01:16:12.071 align:middle line:84%
Email me if you do have any
questions about this stuff.

01:16:12.071 --> 01:16:13.321 align:middle line:90%
I can try to help out.

01:16:13.321 --> 01:16:16.400 align:middle line:90%


01:16:16.400 --> 01:16:19.980 align:middle line:84%
Other than than, good luck on
your game design on this class

01:16:19.980 --> 01:16:21.900 align:middle line:84%
and for the rest of
the semester.

01:16:21.900 --> 01:16:22.860 align:middle line:90%
